# No upper-layer use cases on lower-layer APIs

In any layered architecture, lower layers must not comment on specific callers or flows above them.

Do **not** add JSDoc or inline comments that name upper-layer use cases, product flows, callers, or "pass X when …" scenarios. That couples the lower layer to today's consumers and goes stale when a new one appears.

Applies to repositories, clients, shared libs, utilities, domain services consumed by application/workflow code — any dependency that sits below its callers.

## Do not write

```typescript
/**
 * Used by out-of-scope cleanup to resolve flags whose accounts left the
 * evaluation set.
 */
listActiveKeysBySystemId(...)

/**
 * Pass `["ACTIVE"]` when an admin dismiss must win a race (e.g. out-of-scope
 * cleanup).
 */
states?: readonly FlaggedAccountState[];
```

## Do instead

- Name methods and parameters for what *this* layer does.
- Put caller-specific rationale at the call site only when the constraint is non-obvious — or nowhere if the code already says it.
- Keep lower-layer comments limited to mechanics that layer owns which the signature cannot express (e.g. storage quirks, protocol constraints).
